.TH VERSION 5
.SH NAME
version \- negotiate protocol version
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B size[4] Tversion tag[2] msize[4] version[s]
.B size[4] Rversion tag[2] msize[4] version[s]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Tversion
negotiates the message size and protocol version for a connection,
and must be the first message either side sends.
Its tag is
.BR NOTAG ,
because no tag space exists until it succeeds,
and nothing else may be outstanding while it is.
.PP
.I Msize
is the largest complete message, from the four bytes of
.I size
through the end of the body, either side will send or accept.
The client proposes a ceiling and the server answers with the smaller
of that and its own.
Both sides hold to the answer from then on.
See
.IR intro (5)
for
.IR iounit ,
the number a bulk transfer is actually chunked by.
.PP
Vectra speaks one version,
.BR 9P2000.L ,
exactly.
A
.B Tversion
that does not name it gets an
.B Rversion
whose
.I version
field is the seven characters
.BR unknown ,
not an
.BR Rlerror :
9P's way to say the dialect is refused, rather than that the request
failed.
.PP
A connection performs this handshake once, when it is built.
A program that posts a service in
.B /srv
answers
.B Tversion
before anything else arrives on the pipe;
the kernel gives it half a second to do so before the connection
comes down.
See
.IR mnt (9).
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/vectra9/session.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (5),
.IR attach (5),
.IR vectra9 (2),
.IR mnt (9)
